Not because AI is special, but because AI has the same weakness as a new developer joining a project:

> It understands local context much better than global context.

A human senior engineer can hold a lot of the architecture in their head. An AI is constantly seeing only slices of the codebase. So some structures are dramatically easier for AI to work with.

## AI-friendly codebase characteristics

### 1. Small files

Bad:

```text
page.tsx (1200 lines)
```

Good:

```text
page.tsx (50 lines)
search-console.tsx (150 lines)
location-input.tsx (120 lines)
worker-grid.tsx (180 lines)
```

Why?

When AI opens a file, it can understand the whole thing quickly.

When a file becomes huge, the model starts spending context on navigation instead of reasoning.

---

### 2. Clear ownership

Bad:

```text
page.tsx
- UI
- API calls
- search logic
- geolocation
- state management
```

Good:

```text
page.tsx
  ↓
HeroSection

useGeolocation

workersService
```

Each thing has an obvious owner.

If you ask AI:

> Add caching to worker fetching

it immediately knows where to look.

---

### 3. Feature grouping

Usually better than giant shared folders.

Good:

```text
features/
  search/
  workers/
  certificate/
```

Why?

You can tell AI:

> Only modify files inside features/search

and it won't wander into unrelated code.

---

### 4. Service boundaries

Bad:

```text
fetch(...)
```

scattered everywhere.

Good:

```text
services/workers.ts
services/location.ts
```

Then later:

```text
Switch to Supabase
```

AI modifies one place instead of twenty.

---

### 5. Predictable naming

Bad:

```text
helper.ts
utils2.ts
newStuff.ts
temp.ts
```

AI has to open everything.

Good:

```text
worker-service.ts
location-service.ts
search-console.tsx
```

The filename tells the story.

---

### 6. Explicit types

This is huge.

Bad:

```ts
const worker = {}
```

Good:

```ts
type Worker = {
  id: string
  name: string
  rating: number
}
```

AI becomes far more accurate because the data model is visible.

---

## The biggest AI-friendly trick

Create a root document:

```text
ARCHITECTURE.md
```

with:

```text
Features:
- Search
- Workers
- Messaging (future)

Rules:
- Components render only
- Hooks own state
- Services own data access

Do not fetch directly inside components.
```

Then every AI session starts with:

> Read ARCHITECTURE.md before making changes.

This is probably the highest ROI thing you can do.

